    kind: work_item  # 3515
    title: "DECISION: does a futex (std::sync) or a clock read (std::time) cross an I/O boundary? Four rust modules are unmarkable until this is ruled, and the mpsc-is-not-ipc_recv precedent needs re-examining"  # 3515
    status: needs_human_review  # 3515
    priority: 2  # 3515
    parent:  # 3515
    tags:  # 3515
    - io_boundary  # 3515
    - rust  # 3515
    - vocabulary  # 3515
    isbefore: []  # 3515
    duplicate_of: []  # 3515
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3515
    pr_ref:  # 3515
    description: "DECISION ITEM, not enumeration work. Four of WI-lutuh's 17 unenumerated rust\nmodules refuse for a reason no amount of cataloguing resolves: what they reach\nis a real syscall that maps to NONE of the nine boundary kinds.\n\nMODULES: std::sync, std::sync::Mutex, std::time, std::time::SystemTime.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE, cited rather than argued:\n  std::sync, std::sync::Mutex   `use crate::sys::sync as sys;` (condvar.rs,\n                                nonpoison/mutex.rs) -- futex / condvar. A real\n                                kernel call.\n  std::time, SystemTime         `use crate::sys::{FromInner, IntoInner, time};`\n                                (std/src/time.rs) -- the wall clock. Also a real\n                                kernel call.\n\nTHE NINE BOUNDARY KINDS are fs_read, fs_write, net_send, net_recv, subprocess,\nenv_read, host_info_read, ipc_recv, logging. A futex is none of them. A clock\nread is arguably `host_info_read` -- the boundary INV-tutar split out of env_read\nprecisely to hold host DESCRIPTION reads (hostname, uid, OS, arch) -- but nobody\nhas ruled on whether time is host description.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT BE LEFT IMPLICIT. `module_completeness: complete` asserts that\nevery I/O primitive the module exposes is enumerated. For these four that\nresolves to \"zero, and they are all enumerated\" IF the ruling is that futexes and\nclocks are not boundaries -- which would make all four grantable immediately at\nno cataloguing cost. If the ruling goes the other way, `std::time` needs\nhost_info_read rows and the boundary vocabulary needs a term for synchronisation.\nThe two answers differ by four modules and a vocabulary entry, and refusing by\ndefault costs coverage in an audit whose whole purpose is coverage.\n\nRELATED PRECEDENT the ruling should be consistent with: std::sync::mpsc was\nGRANTED in the same audit on the reasoning that Rust's mpsc is INTRA-process, so\nit is not `ipc_recv` (which names a cross-process boundary). That was a\nvocabulary call made in passing and it should be re-examined alongside this one\nrather than left as an unexamined precedent.\n\nDO NOT GUESS. The standing owner ruling is that stdlib no-I/O is asserted through\nPOSITIVE DATED DECLARATIONS, never through a wider gate, so a silent \"probably\nfine\" here is exactly the shape that ruling forbids."  # 3515
    fields: {}  # 3515
- op: discuss  # a413
  at: '2026-08-26T22:06:14Z'  # a413
  by: agent  # a413
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a413
  clock: 2  # a413
  nonce: a413  # a413
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-26, verbatim: \"yes i think the clock counts as host\ninformation. and I think locks count as a boundary as well.\"\n\nThis resolves both halves of this item, and it goes AGAINST the agent's\nrecommendation, which was that neither is a boundary. Recorded as the owner's\ncall, made with the cost table in front of them.\n\nWHAT IT SETTLES:\n  1. A clock read IS host_info_read. std::time and std::time::SystemTime need\n     catalogue rows, not a no-I/O declaration.\n  2. A lock IS a boundary. std::sync and std::sync::Mutex are NOT declarable as\n     clean either. The nine kinds have no term for synchronisation, so this\n     ruling REQUIRES A TENTH BOUNDARY KIND -- the owner was shown that\n     consequence in the option table and chose it anyway.\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO THE WORK, stated plainly because it points the opposite way\nfrom why this item was filed: WI-pavob was filed to UNSTICK four modules. This\nruling does not unstick them -- it makes all four require MORE work than the\nother twenty-nine, because two need new rows and two need a boundary kind that\ndoes not exist yet. That is a legitimate outcome (the audit exists to be\ncorrect, not to be cheap) but it means WI-lutuh's rust half now has an\narchitectural dependency it did not have this morning.\n\nOPEN AND NOT RULED -- three questions this ruling OPENS rather than closes.\nNone is decided unilaterally; each is put to the owner separately:\n  a. THE NAME AND SHAPE OF THE TENTH KIND. Whether it is one kind covering all\n     synchronisation, or narrower.\n  b. WHETHER mpsc MOVES WITH IT. std::sync::mpsc was GRANTED earlier in this\n     audit on the reasoning that Rust channels are intra-process and therefore\n     not ipc_recv. That reasoning was about ipc_recv specifically and says\n     nothing about a synchronisation kind. A channel IS a synchronisation\n     primitive, so under this ruling the earlier grant is probably wrong and\n     should be revisited rather than left standing as unexamined precedent.\n  c. CROSS-LANGUAGE SCOPE. \"A clock read is host information\" is a statement\n     about the VOCABULARY, not about Rust. If it holds, it holds for python's\n     time.time()/datetime.now(), java's System.currentTimeMillis(), go's\n     time.Now() and every other language's clock -- and none of those carry\n     host_info_read rows today. The same applies to every language's mutex.\n     Ruling it for rust alone would make the vocabulary mean different things\n     per language, which is the failure mode the axis discipline exists to\n     prevent."  # a413
- op: discuss  # 4de4
  at: '2026-08-26T22:06:51Z'  # 4de4
  by: agent  # 4de4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4de4
  clock: 3  # 4de4
  nonce: 4de4  # 4de4
  message: "mpsc RESOLVED BY THE AGENT 2026-08-26, as a DERIVATION from the owner's ruling\nrather than a new policy call. The owner's words: \"std::sync::mpsc I don't know\nabout. I'm not a rust programmer.\" So this is decided on their behalf, recorded\nwith its reasoning, and REVERSIBLE.\n\nWHAT mpsc IS, in plain terms: a channel is a queue INSIDE one program. Several\nthreads put messages in, one thread takes them out. It is passing notes between\nworkers in the same building, not mailing a letter to another building.\n\nTHE DECISION: std::sync::mpsc gets the NEW synchronisation kind, and remains\nNOT ipc_recv. Both are true at once, which is why the earlier grant was not\nsimply wrong:\n\n  * NOT ipc_recv still holds. That kind names a CROSS-PROCESS boundary, and a\n    Rust channel never leaves the process. The original reasoning was correct\n    about the question it was answering.\n  * The new kind DOES apply. When a receiver has nothing to take, it sleeps and\n    asks the kernel to wake it when a value arrives -- the SAME machinery as a\n    contended lock. The owner ruled a lock is a boundary; a blocking channel\n    receive is that same mechanism, so it is that same boundary.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE EARLIER GRANT: std::sync::mpsc was granted\n`module_completeness: complete` in this audit, i.e. declared to expose NO I/O.\nUnder the ruling that is now FALSE -- it exposes the new kind. So the grant must\nbe WITHDRAWN when the tenth kind lands, not merely annotated. Flagged here so it\nis not left standing as an unexamined precedent, which is exactly what this item\nwas filed to prevent.\n\nCONSISTENCY NOTE: the same reasoning applies to every blocking-handoff\nprimitive in every language, not just Rust's channels -- go's channels, python's\nqueue.Queue and threading.Event, java's BlockingQueue. That is part of the\ncross-language scope question (c) above and is not decided here."  # 4de4
